From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 10:19:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 149/185] DRV_INIT does not block, so stop blocking on it

Twenty seconds was indefensible and the dispatcher says why:

  2e26: bclr #7,d0
  2e2a: beq  $2F10      bit 7 clear -> the command queue
  2e2c: bsr  $2E3A      bit 7 set   -> the synchronous jump table

$10 has bit 7 clear. DRV_INIT is queued: $2F10 writes the function
number and one longword of argument into the block at a5+$5AF2, sets
the pending bit, and returns. The body at $313E issues CDCSTOP and then
yields through $2F54 -- it saves its own return address and gets out of
the way. It finishes over the following drive exchanges, on the drive's
schedule, whether or not anybody is watching.

So it does not take twenty seconds. It does not take any time at all,
and neither figure in this file was ever measuring the call. What was
taking twenty seconds was this driver blocking on CDB_STAT inside a
firmware visit -- EmuTOS swapped out, its vectors borrowed, a watchdog
running -- waiting for an asynchronous command to complete. Doom posts
DRV_INIT, posts CDCSETMODE with the next instruction, and goes straight
to a read; it tests CDBSTAT in its frame loop like everything else.

Bring-up now does the same. Post DRV_INIT, post CDCSETMODE 0, return.
EmuTOS has an entire boot left to run and the drive can use it, and the
mount retry added alongside means Getbpb is re-attempted until the disc
answers. No wait, no watchdog exposure, and an empty tray costs nothing.

CDCSETMODE is also new here. Doom calls it two instructions after
DRV_INIT and this driver never has; it is the byte CDCSTART reads to
choose $A7/$F0.
---
 bios/scdapi.h  |  2 +-
 bios/segacd2.S | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 43 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 6916684..3edf26b 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  17
+#define SCD_VERSION  18
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index 1d27ab5..2b6aadb 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -557,6 +557,43 @@ brd_own_done:
         move.w  #0x0010,d0              // DRV_INIT
         move.w  d0,50(a5)
         jsr     0x5F22
+
+        // CDCSETMODE 0, which is what Doom does with the very next
+        // instruction after DRV_INIT and what nothing here has ever
+        // done. It is synchronous and it only stores a mode byte, but
+        // it is the byte CDCSTART reads to choose $A7/$F0.
+        move.w  #0,d1
+        move.w  #0x0096,d0              // CDCSETMODE
+        move.w  d0,50(a5)
+        jsr     0x5F22
+
+        // A count of zero posts the wake and leaves. No wait.
+        //
+        // DRV_INIT is function $10, and the dispatcher splits on bit 7
+        // of the function number:
+        //
+        //   2e26: bclr #7,d0
+        //   2e2a: beq  $2E30      bit 7 clear -> $2F10, the queue
+        //   2e2c: bsr  $2E3A      bit 7 set   -> the sync jump table
+        //
+        // Bit 7 of $10 is clear, so DRV_INIT is queued and the call
+        // returns at once. It does not take twenty seconds; it does not
+        // take any time at all. The drive gets on with spinning up and
+        // reading its table of contents over the following seconds, on
+        // its own schedule, and Doom does exactly that -- posts
+        // DRV_INIT, posts CDCSETMODE, carries straight on, and tests
+        // CDBSTAT in its frame loop.
+        //
+        // What this file was doing instead was blocking on CDB_STAT
+        // inside the visit, with EmuTOS swapped out, its vectors
+        // borrowed and a watchdog running, waiting for an asynchronous
+        // command to finish. That is where the twenty-second budget
+        // came from, and it was twenty seconds of answering the wrong
+        // question. Post it and get out; EmuTOS has a whole boot left
+        // to run and the drive can use it.
+        tst.l   4(a5)
+        beq     brd_posted
+
 brd_wait_init:
         move.l  _segacd_bios_frames,d2
         // How long DRV_INIT gets, and why it is now a variable.
@@ -608,6 +645,11 @@ brd_wait_init:
         beq     brd_init_done
         cmpi.w  #0x0010,d0              // playing, also fine
         bne     brd_wait_init
+brd_posted:
+        move.b  #1,_bios_woken          // asked once; that is enough
+        clr.w   32(a5)                  // stage 0: posted, nothing failed
+        bra     brd_done
+
 brd_init_done:
         // The read gets its own clock. Every stage below tests the same
         // counter against a threshold measured from the start of the
@@ -631,9 +673,6 @@ brd_awake:
         // Nothing on a normal boot ran it, which is why D: has been
         // unusable until the diagnostic was launched by hand. Splitting
         // the wake out of the read is what lets bring-up call it.
-        tst.l   4(a5)
-        beq     brd_done                // stage stays zero: woken, no read
-
         movea.l a5,a0
         move.w  #0x0089,d0              // CDCSTOP
         move.w  d0,50(a5)
